  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThis strategy combines global and local search methods to optimize the overlap integral for the Erd\u0151s problem. It uses a two-phase approach:\n- **Phase 1: Simulated Annealing (SA)** for global exploration to escape local minima\n- **Phase 2: SLSQP optimization** for local refinement of promising solutions\n\nThe method is particularly suited for this task because:\n- SA handles the highly non-convex nature of the problem by allowing occasional uphill moves\n- SA avoids premature convergence by cooling the temperature schedule\n- SLSQP provides rapid local convergence once a good region is found\n- Combined approach balances exploration (SA) with exploitation (SLSQP)\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n- Decision variable: h \u2208 [0,1]^n, where \u2211h[i] = n/2\n- Objective: min_h max_k [h \u22c6 (1 - h)](k)\n- Transition function for SA: h_new = h_current + \u03c3 * T * Z, where Z \u223c N(0,I), \u03c3 = 0.1\n- Temperature schedule: T(t) = T_initial * \u03b1^t, T_initial = 1.0, \u03b1 = 0.995\n- Feasibility repair: clip to [0,1], then scale to meet \u2211h = n/2\n- SA acceptance probability: P = exp(-(f_new - f_old)/T)\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na. Initial solution setup:\n   1. If initial_h_values provided:\n      - Resample to grid size 280 using linear interpolation\n      - Clip to [0,1], then scale to satisfy \u2211h = n/2\n   2. Else:\n      - Create default with central pulse\n      - Normalize and clip\n   3. Set n_points = 280\n\nb. SA initialization:\n   1. Initialize current solution h_current = initial solution\n   2. Compute initial objective f_current = max_overlap(h_current)\n   3. Set T = T_initial, iteration = 0\n   4. Track best solution found so far\n\nc. SA iterative loop:\n   For until budget exhausted:\n   1. Generate candidate h_proposal:\n      - h_proposal = h_current + \u03c3 * T * np.random.randn(n)\n      - Repair feasibility: clip and scale\n   2. Compute f_proposal = max_overlap(h_proposal)\n   3. Compute acceptance probability:\n      - If f_proposal < f_current: accept = 1\n      - Else: accept = exp(-(f_proposal - f_current)/T)\n   4. If accept > random uniform [0,1]:\n      - Update h_current = h_proposal\n      - f_current = f_proposal\n   5. If f_current is best so far:\n      - Update best solution\n      - Decrease temperature faster\n   6. Decrease temperature: T = \u03b1 * T\n   7. Check time budget and terminate early if needed\n\nd. SLSQP refinement:\n   1. Use best solution from SA as initial point\n   2. Optimize using SLSQP with same constraints and objective\n   3. Repair feasibility after each iteration\n   4. Update best solution if improved\n\ne. Final validation:\n   1. Clip and scale best solution to meet constraints\n   2. Compute final objective using exact evaluator\n   3. Return best_h, best_c5, n_points\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- SA population size: 1 (efficient memory usage)\n- Initial temperature T_initial = 1.0\n- Cooling rate \u03b1 = 0.995\n- Maximum SA iterations: 2500\n- SLSQP iterations: 250\n- Mutation scale \u03c3 = 0.1\n- Faster cooling when improvement found: \u03b1_improve = 0.8\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n- SA iterations: ~1000s (most expensive part)\n- SLSQP iterative refinement: ~100s\n- Feasibility repair ensures valid candidates\n- Initial solution maintains feasibility\n- Early termination handles time constraints\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Final solution checked for 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 and \u2211h = n/2\n- Objective computed using exact evaluator\n- Intermediate solutions validated for feasibility\n- Best solution updated only if improved\n- Early termination retains best solution found",
